  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nFor tonight through Wednesday, the primary concern is with the \nstrong shortwave trough that will amplify and cross the ArkLaTex\nand lower MS Valley regions. This system will bring our area \nroughly a 6 to 12 hr window of strong lift. It seems the strongest\nascent and moisture transport will take place in the 3 AM to Noon\ntime frame. Most guidance is now locked in on significant and \nbeneficial rain to fall over central/eastern MS. For locations \neast of the I-55 corridor, probabilistic rainfall forecasts have \nincreased, and now indicate medium to high chances (30 to 70%) \nfor total amounts greater than two inches. As of now, the long \nduration of rainfall suggests we shouldn't see rain rates that \nlead to any flooding problems, but dormant vegetative conditions \ncould lead to some ponding of water fairly quick. \n\nAnother forecast aspect is the much cooler temperatures that will \naccompany the trough and polar high pressure surging southward \nthrough the MS Valley region. Strong cold advection will continue\nWed night and Thanksgiving Day as the surface system exits.\nTemperature forecast will depend heavily on timing of the\nclearing, but it will be very chilly in any case. We should see \ntemperatures dip to their lowest values Friday morning when much\nof the area could see lows in the mid to upper 20s. \n\nLooking ahead to the weekend, the trough will depart the area\nallowing much milder temperatures to return. Global model\nensembles have trended more bullish regarding the next rain \nchances for Sunday as the next Pacific stream trough quickly\napproaches. It is too early to determine if warm advection ahead\nof the system will be sufficient for severe storm potential. We\nwill continue to monitor trends for potential hazard messaging. \n/EC/",
